{
  "an9.41:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 9.41 ",
  "an9.41:0.2": "4. The Great Chapter ",
  "an9.41:0.3": "With the Householder Tapussa ",
  "an9.41:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Mallas, near the Mallian town named Uruvelakappa. ",
  "an9.41:1.2": "Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Uruvelakappa for alms. ",
  "an9.41:1.3": "Then, after the meal, on his return from almsround, he addressed Venerable Ānanda, ",
  "an9.41:1.4": "“Ānanda, you stay right here, while I plunge deep into the Great Wood for the day’s meditation.” ",
  "an9.41:1.5": "“Yes, sir,” Ānanda replied. ",
  "an9.41:1.6": "Then the Buddha plunged deep into the Great Wood and sat at the root of a tree for the day’s meditation. ",
  "an9.41:2.1": "The householder Tapussa went up to Venerable Ānanda, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: ",
  "an9.41:3.1": "“Honorable Ānanda, we are laypeople who enjoy sensual pleasures. We like sensual pleasures, we love them and take joy in them. ",
  "an9.41:3.2": "But renunciation seems like an abyss. ",
  "an9.41:3.3": "I have heard that in this teaching and training there are very young mendicants whose minds leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in renunciation. They see it as peaceful. ",
  "an9.41:3.4": "Renunciation is the dividing line between the multitude and the mendicants in this teaching and training.” ",
  "an9.41:4.1": "“Householder, we should see the Buddha about this matt